hifi-receiver licences
======================

This repository holds hardware, our own code and documents, firmware patches
and a little third-party material. Each part has its own licence. The full
licence texts are in LICENSES/.

Hardware: CERN-OHL-P-2.0 (LICENSES/CERN-OHL-P-2.0.txt)
  boards/     KiCad sources, schematics, layouts, Gerbers, renders and
              reports, except the third-party files named below and the
              scripts (.py, .sh), which are code and MIT
  enclosure/  case CAD sources, including the .py scripts that generate the
              case, and their outputs (STL, 3MF, renders)

Our own code and documents: MIT (the text is at the end of this file and in
LICENSES/MIT.txt)
  app/, bench/, tools/, docs/
  scripts (.py, .sh) under boards/
  firmware/components/hifi/, firmware/board/, firmware/test/, firmware/tools/
  firmware/README.md, firmware/fw.json, firmware/upstream.lock
  everything else in this repository not named in this file

Firmware patches: each change takes the licence of the file it changes
  firmware/patches/      changes to squeezelite-esp32. squeezelite's own
                         files are GPL-3.0-or-later. Many of squeezelite-
                         esp32's ESP32 files carry no licence header; for
                         those, the licence of the component the file sits
                         in decides: cspot and bell are GPL-3.0-or-later,
                         nanopb is zlib, wifi-manager is MIT, and anything
                         else follows squeezelite-esp32's own terms. A file
                         with a header follows its header. New files that a
                         patch adds are ours and MIT.
  firmware/idf-patches/  changes to ESP-IDF v4.4.8. Most of its files are
                         Apache-2.0 (LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt), mbedtls
                         included. lwIP and wpa_supplicant files are
                         BSD-3-Clause. The header of each changed file
                         decides.

Not ours: these keep their own terms
  firmware/third_party/libmad/src/ and .../patches/
              libmad 0.15.1b and the patches to it (Debian's fixes and our
              port changes), GPL-2.0-or-later (its own COPYING; also
              LICENSES/GPL-2.0.txt). Our README.md there is MIT.
  boards/*/lib/aiee.pretty/, boards/*/lib/aiee.3dshapes/, boards/*/lib/aiee.kicad_sym
              footprints, symbols and 3D models made by easyeda2kicad from
              EasyEDA / LCSC part data, as their headers say. The licences
              above do not cover them; they keep whatever terms their
              source sets. Renders of the boards show these 3D models too,
              so a render's CERN-OHL-P grant covers our design, not the
              part models in it.
  boards/*/parts/easyeda/*.json, boards/*/parts/lcsc/*.json,
  boards/*/parts/search/*.json
              part data fetched from EasyEDA and LCSC, kept as a record.
              They keep their source's terms.
  KiCad's stock symbol, footprint and 3D libraries, which the boards also
              use, are CC-BY-SA-4.0 with KiCad's exception that lets you use
              them in your own designs under any licence.
  bench/hifibench/ed25519.py
              follows the Python reference code in RFC 8032 section 6,
              which is under the Simplified BSD licence (BSD-2-Clause,
              copyright the IETF Trust and the RFC's authors). The file is
              BSD-2-Clause (LICENSES/BSD-2-Clause.txt); its header says so.
  docs/review-3/sims/fix/lite_mute/models.inc,
  docs/review-4/sims/als/models_r3.inc
              the 2N3904 transistor model is Fairchild's published SPICE
              model; the rest of each file is ours and MIT.

Firmware builds: GPL-3.0 (LICENSES/GPL-3.0.txt)
  A firmware image built from this repository links squeezelite, so the
  image as a whole is distributed under version 3 of the GNU General Public
  License. squeezelite's own files allow any later version. Our MIT files in
  the image stay MIT, and you may reuse them on their own under MIT.

  No AAC in published images. squeezelite-esp32 links a prebuilt Helix AAC
  decoder (components/codecs/lib/libhelix-aac-sbr.a or libhelix-aac.a),
  which is under RealNetworks' RPSL/RCSL. That licence does not fit with
  the GPL, so an image we publish is built without it, and its build fails
  if "libhelix" appears in the link map.

  No Espressif equalizer in published images. squeezelite-esp32 also links
  components/audio/lib/libesp_processing.a, Espressif's ESP-ADF equalizer.
  It comes as a binary with no source, and ESP-ADF's licence allows its use
  only on Espressif products. Neither fits with the GPL, so a published
  image is built without it (the EQ dropped or replaced by our own), and
  its build fails if "libesp_processing" appears in the link map.

  Corresponding Source. Each published image names its exact source:
  this repository's commit, firmware/upstream.lock (the squeezelite-esp32
  commit and the ESP-IDF version), the patches, the build steps in
  firmware/README.md, and the source of every prebuilt library the image
  links. squeezelite-esp32 ships these codecs as compiled archives in
  components/codecs/lib/:
    libFLAC, libogg, libopus, libvorbisidec (Tremor)
              Xiph.Org libraries, BSD-3-Clause. Source: xiph.org.
    libresample16
              a fixed-point resampler; its origin and licence are not
              recorded upstream and are still being traced.
    libalac, libmad
              not used: our builds compile ALAC (from cspot/bell, Apache-
              2.0) and libmad (firmware/third_party/libmad) from source
              (patches 0021 and 0029).
  An image is published only once the source of each archive it links is
  pinned to the version built and published with it, or the archive is
  built from source or left out.

  ESP-IDF's binary libraries (Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, PHY, Xtensa HAL;
  Apache-2.0) are treated as System Libraries under section 1 of the
  GPL-3.0: they come with the ESP-IDF toolchain for the chip, not with
  this work.

Copyright holders: the hifi-receiver authors (Hive-RSI-Lab). Third-party
files keep their own copyright notices.

